{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of quadratic equations of the form \\(x^2 + ax + b = 0\\) such that if \\(c\\) is a root, then \\(c^2 - 2\\) is also a root. Let's denote the roots of the quadratic equation by \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n\\[p + q = -a \\quad \\text{and} \\quad pq = b.\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) is a root, \\(p^2 - 2\\) must also be a root. Similarly, since \\(q\\) is a root, \\(q^2 - 2\\) must also be a root. Therefore, the roots of the quadratic equation must satisfy the condition that if \\(p\\) is a root, then \\(p^2 - 2\\) is also a root, and the same for \\(q\\).\n\nLet's consider the possible values for \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). If \\(p\\) is a root, then \\(p^2 - 2\\) is also a root. This means that \\(p\\) and \\(p^2 - 2\\) must be the roots of the quadratic equation. Therefore, we have:\n\\[p + (p^2 - 2) = -a \\quad \\text{and} \\quad p(p^2 - 2) = b.\\]\n\n","prefix_end":879,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1184,311,8253,279,1372,315,79151,37906,315,279,1352,17767,87,61,17,488,3859,488,293,284,220,15,57758,1741,429,421,17767,66,57758,374,264,3704,11,1221,17767,66,61,17,481,220,17,57758,374,1083,264,3704,13,6771,594,78064,279,19703,315,279,79151,23606,553,17767,79,57758,323,17767,80,59,568,3216,11401,1915,594,54079,11,582,1414,429,510,78045,79,488,2804,284,481,64,1124,31610,1124,1318,90,437,92,1124,31610,39639,284,293,7110,2533,12549,17767,79,57758,374,264,3704,11,17767,79,61,17,481,220,17,57758,1969,1083,387,264,3704,13,34239,11,2474,17767,80,57758,374,264,3704,11,17767,80,61,17,481,220,17,57758,1969,1083,387,264,3704,13,15277,11,279,19703,315,279,79151,23606,1969,26553,279,2971,429,421,17767,79,57758,374,264,3704,11,1221,17767,79,61,17,481,220,17,57758,374,1083,264,3704,11,323,279,1852,369,17767,80,59,3593,10061,594,2908